import type { SpeakingLongTurnPack } from './speaking-model.js';

export const speakingPart2Packs: SpeakingLongTurnPack[] = [
  { topic: 'A patient explanation at a ticket office', band: 3, cue: 'Describe a person who explained an unfamiliar process patiently to you.', points: ['who the person was', 'what you needed to understand', 'how the person explained it', 'and explain why their help was valuable'], answer: 'I would like to talk about a woman working at a railway ticket office. I met her when I was buying my first ticket for a journey with a change of trains. I knew where I wanted to go, but I did not understand whether one ticket covered both parts of the journey. The online instructions had made me more confused.\n\nThe woman first asked me what time I needed to arrive. Then she drew a small route on a piece of paper and wrote the two departure times underneath it. She pointed out the station where I would change and explained that I should follow the platform number on the screen there. Instead of repeating the information faster, she let me ask each question separately. She also asked me to explain the route back to her, which helped us check that I understood.\n\nHer help was valuable because I stopped feeling embarrassed about travelling alone. I kept her little drawing in my pocket and used it at the connecting station. The trip went smoothly, and afterwards I felt confident enough to organise another train journey without asking a relative to come with me.' },
  { topic: 'A useful second-hand desk', band: 3.5, cue: 'Describe a second-hand item that was useful to you.', points: ['what the item was', 'how you obtained it', 'what condition it was in', 'and explain why it was a good choice'], answer: 'The second-hand item I want to describe is a wooden desk that I bought from a neighbour. She was moving to a smaller flat and put a notice in our building. At that time, I was studying at the dining table, which meant clearing away my books whenever anyone wanted to eat. Her desk looked like a practical solution.\n\nI visited her flat and measured it before agreeing to buy it. There were a few scratches on the top, and one drawer was difficult to open, but the legs were firm. My brother helped me carry it downstairs. We cleaned it, rubbed a little candle wax along the drawer runners and covered the most scratched area with a large desk mat. I did not try to make it look completely new.\n\nIt was a good choice because it fitted into the corner beside my window and cost much less than a new desk. More importantly, I could leave my work arranged properly between study sessions. I began studying for short periods after dinner because getting started no longer involved moving everything around. The desk also reminded me that a useful object does not need to be perfect or expensive.' },
  { topic: 'A surprising school reunion', band: 4, cue: 'Describe a meeting with someone you had not seen for a long time.', points: ['who the person was', 'where the meeting happened', 'what you talked about', 'and explain how you felt after the meeting'], answer: 'Last winter I unexpectedly met a friend from primary school in a supermarket. We had not seen each other since her family moved to another town, so at first I recognised her face without being certain who she was. She recognised me immediately and called my name while I was choosing some vegetables.\n\nWe moved away from the crowded aisle and talked near the entrance. I learned that she had returned to the area to help her parents and was working in a pharmacy. I told her about my studies and where I lived. We also remembered our old classroom, especially a teacher who used to keep a box of unusual stones on her desk. Neither of us remembered all the same details, which made the conversation interesting. We were not simply repeating an identical memory.\n\nAfterwards, I felt pleasantly surprised. I had assumed that a friendship from so long ago would be awkward to restart, but we could still talk quite naturally. We exchanged numbers and met for a short lunch the next week. I do not expect us to become exactly as close as we were as children, but I am glad that a routine shopping trip reopened a connection I thought had disappeared.' },
  { topic: 'A path discovered behind a station', band: 4.5, cue: 'Describe a quiet route that you enjoyed walking along.', points: ['where the route was', 'how you discovered it', 'what you noticed during the walk', 'and explain why you enjoyed it'], answer: 'A quiet route I enjoyed was a path behind the railway station in a town I visited for a training day. I had arrived early and wanted somewhere to walk before going to the course. A sign beside the station pointed towards a riverside footpath, so I followed it instead of waiting inside.\n\nThe path began between two ordinary buildings and then opened onto a narrow river. There were willow trees along one side and small vegetable gardens on the other. I noticed a man repairing a fence and several ducks moving slowly against the current. The path was not empty, but the few people there seemed unhurried. A woman walking a dog nodded to me, and two cyclists passed without making the walk feel crowded.\n\nI enjoyed the route because it gave me a calmer start to the day. I could still hear an occasional train, so I knew I was close to the station, yet the busy street felt far away. I walked for about twenty minutes and returned with enough time for a drink before the course. Later, I saved the location because I would happily use the same path if I ever had another appointment in that town.' },
  { topic: 'Solving a recipe misunderstanding', band: 5, cue: 'Describe a time when a small mistake helped you learn something.', points: ['what you were trying to do', 'what mistake you made', 'how you dealt with it', 'and explain what you learned'], answer: 'I made a useful small mistake while preparing a soup for my family. I was following a recipe that a friend had sent me, and I read the quantity of salt too quickly. Instead of adding a teaspoon, I added a tablespoon. I realised the mistake as soon as I tasted the soup, but by then the salt had already dissolved.\n\nAt first I wanted to throw the whole pot away. My father suggested that we think about the other ingredients before deciding. We cooked another small batch without any salt, added extra vegetables and combined the two. There was more soup than we needed, but it tasted normal and we froze some for another evening. I also checked the original recipe and saw that the abbreviation had been clear; I had simply assumed I knew what it meant.\n\nThe experience taught me to read measurements carefully rather than rushing through familiar steps. It also showed me that solving a mistake can be more useful than immediately starting again. I now put the measured ingredients into small bowls before cooking a new dish. That takes a few extra minutes, but I feel less hurried and can concentrate on the actual cooking.' },
  { topic: 'A room made brighter with a small change', band: 5.5, cue: 'Describe a small change you made to a room that improved it.', points: ['which room it was', 'what you changed', 'how you made the change', 'and explain what difference it made'], answer: 'I made a small change to the room where I study by moving a tall shelf away from the window. The room had felt dark for months, and I kept thinking I needed a stronger lamp. One afternoon I noticed that the shelf was blocking light from the lower part of the window, even though it did not cover the glass completely.\n\nBefore moving anything, I measured the opposite wall and checked where the electrical sockets were. My partner helped me empty the shelf and slide it across the room. We kept heavy books on the lower shelves and moved a chair into the space beside the window. The whole job took less than an hour, and we did not buy any new furniture. I also found several papers I had forgotten between the books.\n\nThe difference was immediate. The desk received more daylight, and the room felt wider because the entrance no longer faced a large block of furniture. I began using the chair for reading in the afternoon instead of going into the living room. What pleased me most was that the improvement came from observing the room carefully rather than spending money. It made me more willing to try simple changes before assuming that a problem required something new.' },
  { topic: 'An interesting local history talk', band: 6, cue: 'Describe a talk that changed how you saw a familiar place.', points: ['where you heard the talk', 'what the speaker discussed', 'which detail interested you', 'and explain how your view of the place changed'], answer: 'I attended a short talk about the old railway bridge near my hometown. It took place in a community hall, and the speaker was a retired engineer who had helped inspect the bridge many years earlier. I had crossed it frequently, but I had never considered it more than an ordinary part of the route into town.\n\nHe explained how the bridge had been built in stages because the riverbed was difficult to survey. He showed photographs of temporary wooden structures and described the workers carrying equipment before there was a permanent crossing. The detail that interested me most was a change to the design after local boat operators complained that one opening would be too narrow. It demonstrated that the finished structure reflected practical negotiation, not just one person’s original plan.\n\nAfter the talk, I looked at the bridge differently. The supports and unusual spacing, which I had barely noticed before, became evidence of decisions made by real people. I returned the next weekend and compared the present view with a photograph from the talk. I would not claim that I became an expert in engineering, but I gained a much stronger appreciation of the effort hidden inside a familiar feature of the town.' },
  { topic: 'A thoughtful message after an interview', band: 6.5, cue: 'Describe a message that encouraged you at a difficult time.', points: ['who sent the message', 'what was happening at the time', 'what the message said', 'and explain why it encouraged you'], answer: 'The message came from a former classmate after I had an interview for a job I really wanted. I thought the interview had gone badly because I struggled to answer one question clearly. On the way home I sent her a brief message saying that I probably would not get the position.\n\nShe did not reply with a promise that everything would be fine. Instead, she reminded me of a project we had completed together and described how I had stayed calm when our original plan failed. She said that one uncertain answer did not erase the skills I had already shown, and she suggested writing down what I would explain differently next time. Her message was specific enough that I knew she was responding to me, rather than sending a general motivational phrase.\n\nIt encouraged me because it gave me something practical to do without dismissing my disappointment. I made notes that evening and realised that several other answers had actually been clear. I did not get that particular job, but I used the notes in a later interview and felt better prepared. I still value the message because it combined kindness with an honest perspective, rather than treating confidence as something I could simply switch on.' },
  { topic: 'A public garden visited in winter', band: 7, cue: 'Describe a place that you enjoyed outside its busiest season.', points: ['where the place was', 'when you visited it', 'how it differed from a busy-season visit', 'and explain what you enjoyed about that difference'], answer: 'I visited a public garden on the edge of the city in the middle of winter. I had been there once during a spring festival, when the paths were full of visitors and almost every conversation involved taking photographs of flowers. This time, a friend suggested going after a morning of light rain.\n\nThe garden was much quieter and there were very few flowers, but it did not feel empty of interest. Without the leaves, we could see the shape of the branches and the small birds moving between them. The gardener had placed labels beside dormant plants, which helped us understand what would appear later in the year. We also noticed the drainage channels along a path, something that had been hidden by crowds on my earlier visit.\n\nI enjoyed having time to observe rather than feeling that I should hurry towards the next colourful display. We could sit under a shelter and listen to water dripping from the roof without holding up other visitors. The visit reminded me that a place can be worthwhile without looking like its promotional photographs. I still appreciate the spring flowers, but winter showed me the structure and maintenance that make the more spectacular season possible.' },
  { topic: 'A discussion that improved a group plan', band: 7.5, cue: 'Describe a discussion in which people reached a better decision together.', points: ['what decision was being made', 'who participated', 'how the discussion developed', 'and explain why the final decision was better'], answer: 'I remember a discussion about organising a small fundraising event at our college. Our group initially wanted to hold a large evening concert because it sounded exciting and might attract attention. Five of us met to divide the work, but one member asked a question that changed the direction: who would actually be able to attend?\n\nShe pointed out that several students travelled a long distance and that local families might not want to come home late. Another member explained that hiring sound equipment would absorb much of the money we hoped to raise. Instead of defending our first idea, we listed what the event needed to achieve and compared three alternatives. We eventually chose an afternoon activity market with a short acoustic performance, which required less equipment and allowed people to come for only part of the event.\n\nThe final decision was better because it matched the audience and our resources. We raised a useful amount without taking on costs we could not confidently cover. More importantly, the discussion gave each person a reason to support the plan. I learned that reaching agreement does not have to mean splitting the difference between opinions; sometimes the most useful step is asking a different question about the purpose of the decision.' },
  { topic: 'An object with an unexpected story', band: 8, cue: 'Describe an ordinary object whose history interested you.', points: ['what the object was', 'where you saw it', 'what you learned about its history', 'and explain why that history interested you'], answer: 'The object was a battered metal lunch box in my grandmother’s cupboard. I had seen it many times without paying attention, and I assumed she kept it simply because it was still usable. One afternoon, while helping her clear the cupboard, I asked why there was a small number scratched into the lid.\n\nShe explained that my grandfather had used it when working at a factory. Several workers had identical boxes, so each marked one to avoid taking someone else’s lunch. Later, when the factory closed, he used the box to carry seeds to a shared garden. My grandmother showed me a photograph in which it was just visible beside a row of young plants. The dents were therefore connected with two very different stages of his working life.\n\nThat history interested me because it challenged how quickly I judge ordinary possessions. The box was neither beautiful nor rare, but it contained evidence of routine, friendship and adaptation after a difficult change. We decided not to polish it until it looked new, since the marks were part of the story. I wrote down my grandmother’s explanation, not to make the object seem more valuable, but so the reason for keeping it would not disappear when someone else eventually opened that cupboard.' },
  { topic: 'Helping a visitor use public transport', band: 3, cue: 'Describe a time when you helped a visitor find their way.', points: ['who needed help', 'where they wanted to go', 'what you did to help', 'and explain how you knew the help was useful'], answer: 'I helped a visitor outside a bus station near my college. He was holding a paper map and looking at the stop numbers. He asked me whether a bus from that station went to the botanical garden. I knew the route because I had visited the garden with my family a few weeks earlier.\n\nFirst, I checked the destination printed on the timetable rather than relying only on memory. I showed him the correct stop and explained that he would need to stay on the bus for about twenty minutes. The garden stop had a different name from the garden itself, so I wrote that name on his map. I also pointed out a tall red building just before the stop, which would give him a visible warning that he was nearly there.\n\nHe seemed relieved and repeated the stop name to make sure he could recognise it. I waited nearby until the bus arrived and showed him which door to use. He thanked me through the window as the bus left. The help was small, but it reminded me how confusing an ordinary journey can feel when the place is unfamiliar. I would appreciate the same clear explanation if I were visiting another city.' },
  { topic: 'A new activity tried at a community centre', band: 3.5, cue: 'Describe an activity you tried for the first time in a group.', points: ['what the activity was', 'who was in the group', 'what happened during the session', 'and explain whether you would try it again'], answer: 'I tried a basic pottery activity at a community centre during an open afternoon. I went with a friend, and there were about eight people at our table, including two parents and their teenage children. None of us had much experience, so the instructor began by showing how to make a small bowl without using a wheel.\n\nWe each received a piece of clay and a few simple tools. I pressed too hard at first and made one side of my bowl much thinner than the other. The instructor showed me how to support it from underneath, then let me correct it myself. People at the table compared their different shapes, and the conversation made mistakes feel less serious. My finished bowl was uneven, but I added a small line pattern around the edge and felt pleased that it stayed upright.\n\nI would try the activity again because it required concentration in a different way from my usual work. I could not keep checking my phone with clay on my hands, which was actually pleasant. I would not expect to make something impressive immediately, but another session might help me understand the material and enjoy the process rather than worrying about producing a perfect object.' },
  { topic: 'A journey planned with a limited budget', band: 4, cue: 'Describe a trip that you organised without spending much money.', points: ['where you went', 'who travelled with you', 'how you kept the cost down', 'and explain what made the trip enjoyable'], answer: 'I organised a day trip to a coastal town with two friends when we were students. We wanted a break after our exams, but none of us could afford a hotel or an expensive restaurant. The town was close enough to reach by an early train and return in the evening.\n\nWe compared the train times and bought the cheaper tickets a few days ahead. Instead of paying for several attractions, we chose a long walk along the shore and a small free exhibition about local fishing. Each person brought part of a picnic, so we had enough food without one person paying for everything. We did buy hot drinks when the wind became cold, because saving money did not mean making the day uncomfortable.\n\nThe enjoyable part was having very little pressure to follow a busy schedule. We watched boats entering the harbour, ate on a sheltered bench and spent time taking photographs of each other. We missed one train home because we stopped to listen to a street musician, but the next train was only half an hour later. The trip showed me that careful planning can create freedom: by settling the main costs beforehand, we could relax and enjoy the ordinary things around us.' },
  { topic: 'A recommendation that suited you', band: 4.5, cue: 'Describe a recommendation you received that turned out to be useful.', points: ['what was recommended', 'who made the recommendation', 'why you decided to try it', 'and explain why it suited you'], answer: 'A colleague recommended a short walking route to me when I mentioned feeling tired after work. I had been trying to exercise at home, but I usually gave up after a few days because the routine felt repetitive. She suggested a circular route that began near our office and passed through a small residential garden.\n\nI decided to try it because it did not require joining a gym or travelling somewhere special. She explained where the entrance was and warned me that one gate closed early in winter. The first time, I followed the route slowly to check the distance. It took around twenty-five minutes, which was short enough to fit between finishing work and catching my normal bus.\n\nThe recommendation suited me because it connected exercise with a part of the day that already existed. I did not have to change clothes or prepare equipment. The route also had enough variety to keep me interested: a shaded street, an open garden and a view across a playing field. I started doing it twice a week, sometimes with another colleague. I appreciated that my friend recommended something realistic for my schedule instead of an activity she enjoyed that would have been difficult for me to maintain.' },
  { topic: 'A photograph that caught an unnoticed moment', band: 5, cue: 'Describe a photograph that shows something you did not notice at the time.', points: ['when and where the photograph was taken', 'what it shows', 'what you noticed later', 'and explain why the photograph matters to you'], answer: 'The photograph was taken at a family picnic beside a lake two summers ago. My cousin took it while several of us were putting food on a folding table. At first it looked like an ordinary picture of a slightly disorganised lunch, with bags on the ground and people reaching across one another. I did not think much about it when she sent it to our family group.\n\nA few weeks later, I opened the picture on a larger screen and noticed my grandfather in the background. He was showing my youngest niece how to fold a paper boat. I had been busy finding plates and had not seen that moment at all. Her expression was very serious, while he was smiling at the uneven fold she had made. The lake and food became less important to me than those two people in one corner.\n\nThe photograph matters because it preserved an interaction that was not arranged for the camera. My grandfather does not enjoy posing, so we have few relaxed pictures of him. I printed this one and gave him a copy, which led to another conversation about games from his childhood. It reminds me that a good photograph can reveal something valuable after the occasion, rather than simply confirming what everyone already noticed.' },
  { topic: 'An unexpected role in a school performance', band: 5.5, cue: 'Describe a time when you took on a role you had not expected.', points: ['what the situation was', 'which role you took', 'how you prepared for it', 'and explain what you gained from the experience'], answer: 'I took an unexpected role during a small performance at college. I had agreed to help arrange chairs and move equipment, so I assumed I would remain behind the scenes. Two days before the event, the student who was supposed to introduce each act became ill. Our organiser asked whether I could do the introductions instead.\n\nI was hesitant because speaking into a microphone felt very different from helping with furniture. However, the introductions were short, and the organiser offered to practise with me. I wrote a simple card for each act, checked everyone’s names and rehearsed in the empty hall. We discovered that I spoke too quickly when nervous, so I marked places to pause. I also met the performers briefly, which made introducing them feel less like reading about strangers.\n\nOn the evening itself, I forgot one sentence but remembered the person’s name and the main information. Nobody seemed troubled by the missing phrase. The experience gave me confidence that preparation could be practical rather than perfect. I did not suddenly become someone who loved public speaking, but I stopped assuming that nerves meant I should refuse every visible role. Afterwards, I was still happy to move chairs; I simply knew I could contribute in another way when needed.' },
  { topic: 'A small exhibition of everyday work', band: 6, cue: 'Describe an exhibition about a subject you had previously overlooked.', points: ['where the exhibition was', 'what its subject was', 'what you learned there', 'and explain why it held your interest'], answer: 'I visited an exhibition about street maintenance in a public building near the town centre. I had gone there to renew a document, and the display was in the waiting area. It consisted of photographs, old tools and short recordings of workers describing their routines. Street maintenance was not a subject I would normally choose for an afternoon out.\n\nThe display explained why repairing a surface sometimes requires closing a road even when the damaged area looks small. I learned that water below the surface can cause a larger problem than the visible crack. One worker described checking drains before heavy rain and said that a blocked entrance could affect several streets. A photograph showed the same junction before and after improvements, but the labels also acknowledged disruption during the work.\n\nIt held my interest because it made an invisible system understandable. I had previously noticed maintenance mainly when it delayed my journey. Seeing the planning and physical work behind it made that reaction seem rather limited. The exhibition did not suggest that every road closure was perfectly managed, which helped it feel credible. I left with a better understanding of why ordinary public spaces need continuous care and why judging that care only by the inconvenience of one day can be misleading.' },
  { topic: 'Choosing a healthier break at work', band: 6.5, cue: 'Describe a change you made to the way you take a break.', points: ['what your previous habit was', 'why you wanted to change it', 'what you did differently', 'and explain whether the change helped'], answer: 'I changed my lunchtime break after noticing that I often returned to work feeling less refreshed than before. My usual habit was to eat at my desk while scrolling through news and messages. Although I was not doing paid work, I was still looking at the same screen and sitting in the same position.\n\nI decided to test a different routine for two weeks rather than make an ambitious permanent promise. I ate in a small shared kitchen and then walked around the block without listening to anything. The first few days felt strange because I kept reaching for my phone. I eventually left it in my bag, which removed the need to resist checking it every minute. If it rained, I stood near a covered entrance and watched the street for a short time instead.\n\nThe change helped, but not in a dramatic way. I still felt tired after a difficult morning, yet I found it easier to begin the afternoon’s first task. I also spoke briefly to colleagues I rarely saw at my desk. What mattered was treating a break as a change of activity, rather than simply replacing one stream of information with another. I now use the routine most days, while allowing flexibility when a practical reason makes it inconvenient.' },
  { topic: 'A decision to repair a damaged bag', band: 7, cue: 'Describe a time when you repaired something rather than replaced it.', points: ['what needed repair', 'how you decided what to do', 'what the repair involved', 'and explain why you were pleased or disappointed with the result'], answer: 'I decided to repair a canvas bag when its shoulder strap began to separate from the seam. I used it for work, and apart from that damage it was in good condition. My first reaction was to look for a replacement, but every suitable bag cost more than I wanted to spend.\n\nI took it to a small repair shop to ask whether the fabric could still hold a new stitch. The repairer examined the worn area and explained that simply sewing along the old line would probably fail again. He suggested adding a short reinforcing patch underneath the strap. I agreed after checking that the patch would not obstruct the inside pocket. The repair took a day, and the stitching was visible but neat.\n\nI was pleased because the result made the bag useful again without pretending nothing had happened to it. I had already become accustomed to the arrangement of its pockets, so keeping it also saved the inconvenience of adapting to another bag. The experience did not convince me that every damaged item should be repaired regardless of cost. It did encourage me to ask for an informed opinion before assuming replacement was the only sensible option, especially when the rest of an object still served its purpose.' },
  { topic: 'A friend who noticed your progress', band: 7.5, cue: 'Describe a person who noticed an improvement you had made.', points: ['who the person was', 'what you had been working on', 'how they recognised the improvement', 'and explain why their reaction mattered'], answer: 'A friend noticed that I had become more confident explaining ideas in English during an informal study meeting. We had practised together for several months, and I usually relied on written notes whenever the conversation moved beyond familiar daily topics. I was particularly concerned about losing my place halfway through an explanation.\n\nOne evening, we were discussing whether our study group should change its meeting time. I explained the advantages of an earlier start, responded to a concern and suggested a compromise without looking at my notebook. Afterwards, my friend mentioned that I had kept the discussion moving even when I did not know an exact word. She gave a specific example of how I had rephrased one idea rather than stopping completely.\n\nHer reaction mattered because I had been judging progress mainly by the mistakes I still made. From that perspective, improvement was difficult to see: there was always another grammar point or unfamiliar phrase to worry about. She noticed a practical change in how I handled communication. I did not interpret the comment as proof that I had reached a particular exam score. Instead, it helped me recognise a skill that testing individual words would not show, and it encouraged me to continue practising real explanations rather than only memorising lists.' },
  { topic: 'A place transformed by evening light', band: 8, cue: 'Describe a familiar place that looked different at an unusual time of day.', points: ['what the place was', 'when you saw it', 'how its appearance was different', 'and explain the impression it made on you'], answer: 'The place was a pedestrian square that I normally cross in the morning. It sits between office buildings and a small theatre, and during the day I see it mainly as an efficient route to the bus stop. One evening I passed through it just after sunset because a meeting had finished much later than expected.\n\nThe office windows were lit from inside, while the stone paving reflected a soft blue from the remaining daylight. The theatre had switched on warm lights around its entrance, and a few people were talking quietly on the steps. Features I usually overlooked became more noticeable, including the curved edge of a low wall and a sculpture that seemed rather plain in harsh sunlight. The square also sounded different without the morning traffic beyond it.\n\nThe impression was unexpectedly peaceful. Nothing important had physically changed, yet the combination of light and activity gave the place a different character. I stood for a minute instead of crossing automatically and realised how strongly my routine shaped what I thought I knew about it. Since then, I occasionally take the same route in the evening. I am not looking for a spectacular scene; I simply appreciate being reminded that a familiar place can offer a new experience when the circumstances of seeing it change.' },
  { topic: 'Learning to use a sewing machine', band: 3, cue: 'Describe a practical skill you learned with help from a family member.', points: ['what the skill was', 'who helped you', 'how you practised', 'and explain how you have used the skill'], answer: 'My aunt helped me learn the basics of using a sewing machine. I wanted to shorten a pair of trousers, but I had never used her machine and was worried about damaging both the clothes and the equipment. She suggested that I practise on spare fabric before touching the trousers.\n\nShe first showed me how to thread the machine and keep my fingers safely away from the needle. Then she drew straight lines on some old cotton and asked me to sew slowly along them. My first lines were uneven because I kept looking at the needle instead of the guide beside it. She explained the difference and let me repeat the exercise several times. After that, we practised turning a corner and securing the ends of the thread.\n\nI eventually shortened the trousers with her watching, and the result was good enough to wear. Since then, I have used the machine to make simple cloth bags and repair a cushion cover. I still ask for help when the fabric is thin or slippery. The skill has been useful because I can make small changes myself, and I enjoy knowing how something is put together rather than treating every damaged seam as the end of an item.' },
  { topic: 'An enjoyable journey during a power cut', band: 3.5, cue: 'Describe an unexpected situation that led to a pleasant experience.', points: ['what happened unexpectedly', 'where you were', 'what you did as a result', 'and explain why the experience became pleasant'], answer: 'An unexpected power cut led to a pleasant evening walk with my brother. We were at home preparing to watch a film when the lights went out. At first we waited, assuming the electricity would return quickly, but a message from the building manager said the repair might take several hours.\n\nInstead of sitting in the hot flat, we took a torch and walked to a nearby river path. The buildings on our side of the street were dark, but the path’s public lighting was still working. We bought two cold drinks from a stall and found a bench facing the water. Without the planned film, there was no particular subject we needed to discuss. We talked about his new job, a relative’s garden and several ordinary things we had not mentioned during the week.\n\nThe experience became pleasant because the interruption removed our usual distractions. We had spent many evenings in the same room without really talking, since each of us was occupied with a screen. When we returned, the electricity was back, but we no longer wanted to start a long film. We made a simple meal instead. I would not wish for another power cut, yet I was glad that we chose an enjoyable alternative rather than treating the whole evening as lost.' },
  { topic: 'A helpful item borrowed for a move', band: 4, cue: 'Describe something you borrowed that made a task easier.', points: ['what you borrowed', 'who lent it to you', 'what task you used it for', 'and explain how you looked after or returned it'], answer: 'I borrowed a small folding trolley from a neighbour when moving books to a new flat. We were only moving to another building on the same street, so hiring a large vehicle seemed unnecessary. However, after carrying two boxes by hand, I realised the books were heavier than I had expected.\n\nMy neighbour saw us resting by the entrance and offered the trolley he used for market shopping. He showed me how to lock the handle and warned me not to load boxes above the top rail. We placed the heaviest box at the bottom, secured everything with a strap and made several manageable trips instead of one dangerous stack. The trolley’s wheels handled the pavement well, and I could still see clearly in front of me.\n\nAfter the move, I wiped the wheels and checked that the folding mechanism worked normally. I returned it that evening with a short thank-you note. My neighbour said he was glad it had been useful and did not need a reward. The loan made the task easier physically, but it also introduced us properly. Before then, we had only exchanged greetings. I now know that asking or accepting a modest amount of help can create a friendly connection without becoming a large obligation.' },
  { topic: 'A news story about a local discovery', band: 4.5, cue: 'Describe a news story about your local area that interested you.', points: ['what the story was about', 'where you heard or read it', 'which details interested you', 'and explain what you did after learning about it'], answer: 'I read a local news story about an old mosaic discovered underneath the floor of a building being renovated. The article appeared on the town newspaper’s website, and a friend sent me the link because the building was on a street we often used. Until then, I had thought it was an ordinary empty shop.\n\nThe story explained that workers had uncovered the mosaic while removing a later layer of flooring. Specialists were examining it before deciding what could safely be preserved. I was interested in the photographs showing how a small exposed corner led to finding a much larger pattern. The article also made clear that the date was not yet certain, which was more helpful than presenting an exciting guess as a fact.\n\nAfter reading it, I walked past the building and noticed details around the entrance that I had previously ignored. I also followed a later update explaining that the owners hoped to make part of the floor visible through a protected panel. The story interested me because it showed that local history can still be discovered in familiar places. It also reminded me to distinguish between what people have found and what they can confidently explain about it.' },
  { topic: 'A family custom explained to a friend', band: 5, cue: 'Describe a family custom that you explained to someone outside your family.', points: ['what the custom is', 'who you explained it to', 'what you told them about it', 'and explain why the custom matters to you'], answer: 'I explained our family’s first-meal custom to a friend who came to stay during a holiday. Before we begin the main meal, the youngest person at the table chooses one thing to thank someone else for. It does not have to be serious; a child might thank an uncle for fixing a toy or a cousin for sharing a snack.\n\nMy friend looked slightly uncertain when everyone paused before eating, so I explained how it had started. My grandmother introduced it when several relatives lived far apart and only met a few times a year. She wanted the meal to begin with something personal rather than a discussion about work or travel problems. I told my friend that visitors could join in but were not expected to make a speech. She thanked my mother for explaining the ingredients in the food, which fitted the custom perfectly.\n\nIt matters to me because it directs attention towards small acts that might otherwise go unnoticed. We do not use it to avoid disagreements or pretend that every family gathering is easy. It simply creates a warm beginning and gives younger people a voice at a table where adults often dominate the conversation. Seeing my friend participate also helped me notice how unusual, and how valuable, this ordinary family habit had become.' },
  { topic: 'A challenging swim completed gradually', band: 5.5, cue: 'Describe a physical challenge you completed by building up gradually.', points: ['what the challenge was', 'why you wanted to attempt it', 'how you prepared', 'and explain how you felt when you completed it'], answer: 'My challenge was to swim ten lengths of the local pool without taking a long break. I could swim, but I usually stopped after two lengths because I became breathless. I wanted to improve so that swimming could become a regular activity rather than something I did only on holiday.\n\nI began by asking a pool instructor to watch my technique briefly. She noticed that I lifted my head too much and wasted energy. After practising a calmer breathing pattern, I set a modest target of three lengths, then added another length only when that felt comfortable. I went twice a week and wrote down how I felt afterwards instead of comparing myself with faster swimmers. On some days I repeated the same distance because I was tired, which initially felt disappointing but prevented me from pushing too hard.\n\nWhen I finally completed ten lengths, I felt pleased mainly because the effort had become controlled. I was still slow, and several people passed me, but I did not feel panicked about my breathing. The challenge taught me that improvement could come from a better method as well as more determination. I now enjoy swimming more because I can recognise a realistic next step without treating every session as a test of willpower.' },
  { topic: 'A portable lamp during evening study', band: 6, cue: 'Describe a simple item that improved your daily routine.', points: ['what the item is', 'how you began using it', 'what problem it solved', 'and explain why you value it'], answer: 'The item is a small rechargeable reading lamp with an adjustable arm. I began using it when my partner started working earlier shifts and needed to sleep before I finished studying. Our main room light was bright enough for my books but made it difficult for her to rest, while the old desk lamp threw light across most of the room.\n\nI chose the portable lamp after checking how narrowly its light could be directed. I placed it low beside my notebook and adjusted the angle so the page was illuminated without shining towards the bed. It also had a softer setting, which was enough for reading but not so strong that the whole room felt awake. Charging it during breakfast became a simple part of the routine, and there were no loose cables across the floor at night.\n\nI value it because it solved a small shared-space problem without requiring either of us to abandon our schedule. We no longer had to negotiate the same issue every evening. The lamp also made my study area feel more focused, so I was less tempted to spread papers around the room. It is not an impressive piece of technology, but its usefulness comes from fitting a specific need rather than having many functions I would never use.' },
  { topic: 'A book finished during short journeys', band: 6.5, cue: 'Describe a book that you read in an unusual or practical way.', points: ['what kind of book it was', 'when and where you read it', 'how you fitted reading into your routine', 'and explain what you gained from it'], answer: 'I read a collection of short essays about ordinary objects during my bus journeys to work. I had been complaining that I never had time to finish a book, although I spent almost forty minutes travelling most mornings. A friend lent me this collection because each essay could be read independently.\n\nI kept the book in the outer pocket of my bag and read one essay whenever I had a seat. If the bus was crowded or the road made me feel uncomfortable, I left it closed rather than forcing myself to read. I placed a small slip of paper after each essay and wrote a word that reminded me of its subject, such as a chair, a key or a kitchen bowl. That helped me connect the readings across several weeks.\n\nWhat I gained was not simply the satisfaction of finishing a book. The essays encouraged me to notice how everyday objects shape behaviour, and I began looking at my own home more carefully. The method also changed my idea of reading time. I had assumed it required a long quiet evening, which rarely happened. Short journeys were not suitable for every book, but they provided a realistic way to enjoy this one without treating reading as another demanding task.' },
  { topic: 'An online conversation across generations', band: 7, cue: 'Describe a conversation that helped you understand another generation.', points: ['who you spoke with', 'what you discussed', 'what surprised you', 'and explain how the conversation affected your understanding'], answer: 'I had an online conversation with my uncle about his first job. It began because I was preparing a short presentation about changes in working life and wanted a personal example rather than relying entirely on articles. He lives in another city, so we arranged a video call one Sunday afternoon.\n\nI expected him to describe a world with fewer choices and less useful technology. Instead, he talked about learning informally from experienced colleagues and feeling considerable freedom once he was trusted with a task. He also described the disadvantages: finding reliable information was slower, and leaving a bad employer could be difficult when jobs were scarce locally. What surprised me was how carefully he balanced the good and bad aspects. He did not present the past as either better or worse in every respect.\n\nThe conversation affected my understanding by making broad generational labels seem less useful. A person’s experience depended on their workplace, location and responsibilities, not only the decade in which they began working. I used a short quotation in my presentation with his permission, but I also explained that one interview could not represent everyone. Afterwards, we continued talking about things unrelated to the presentation. Asking about his work had opened a more personal conversation than our usual brief exchanges about family news.' },
  { topic: 'A plan changed to include someone', band: 7.5, cue: 'Describe a time when you changed a plan so that another person could take part.', points: ['what the original plan was', 'who might have been excluded', 'what change you made', 'and explain the effect of the change'], answer: 'Our friends initially planned a birthday outing that involved climbing a steep path to a hilltop restaurant. One member of the group had recently injured her ankle, and she said she would simply miss the outing rather than slow everyone down. Although she sounded relaxed about it, I felt that celebrating together should matter more than preserving the exact route.\n\nI suggested choosing a café beside a flatter lakeside path instead. Before proposing it to everyone, I checked whether the entrance had steps and whether there was a nearby bus stop. I also asked our injured friend privately what distance would actually be comfortable, so the alternative was based on her needs rather than our assumptions. The birthday guest liked the lakeside option, and nobody had paid for the original booking yet.\n\nThe change made the afternoon more inclusive without turning the injury into the main subject of the celebration. Some people walked farther around the lake while others sat and talked, so we did not all have to do the same activity continuously. I learned that inclusion often depends on small practical details rather than a dramatic gesture. It also matters to offer a choice respectfully: our friend could decide what suited her instead of being treated as a problem the group had to solve.' },
  { topic: 'A museum label that raised a question', band: 8, cue: 'Describe something you read in a public place that made you think.', points: ['where you read it', 'what the text was about', 'which question it raised for you', 'and explain how you responded to it'], answer: 'I read a label beside a repaired ceramic bowl in a small museum. The bowl had visible joins where broken pieces had been reassembled, and the label explained that conservators had deliberately left some gaps rather than inventing missing patterns. It was a short text, but it made me pause longer than the more elaborate displays nearby.\n\nThe question it raised was how much restoration helps us understand an object and when it begins to hide uncertainty. Before that visit, I had assumed the aim was always to make something look complete. The label described a different aim: showing both the surviving material and the limits of the available evidence. I looked closely at the plain supporting sections and realised that they allowed the original decoration to be read without pretending it had all survived.\n\nAfterwards, I asked a volunteer whether the same principle applied to paintings. She explained that approaches vary according to the material and purpose of a display, which prevented me from turning one example into a universal rule. I found the experience valuable because it linked a practical design decision with intellectual honesty. It also made me more attentive to what public explanations leave uncertain, rather than judging their quality only by how confidently they tell a story.' },
  { topic: 'A child’s question that was difficult to answer', band: 3, cue: 'Describe a question from a child that made you stop and think.', points: ['who asked the question', 'what they asked', 'how you answered', 'and explain why you remember the conversation'], answer: 'My niece asked me why the moon seemed to follow our car. She was sitting in the back while we travelled home after visiting relatives. The moon was bright, and she had been watching it between buildings for several minutes before asking the question. I remembered wondering the same thing as a child, but I could not immediately give a clear explanation.\n\nI told her that the moon was very far away, so our small movement on the road did not change where it appeared as much as nearby trees did. To make that idea clearer, I asked her to watch a lamp beside the road and then look back at the moon. She noticed that the lamp disappeared behind us quickly while the moon remained in almost the same direction. I avoided adding details I was not sure about and promised we could find a simple picture later.\n\nI remember the conversation because her ordinary observation made me look again at something I usually ignored. At home, we found an explanation with a diagram, and she drew a car underneath a large moon. Her question reminded me that giving a useful answer is not the same as using technical words. Sometimes a concrete comparison and an honest limit to your knowledge are more helpful.' },
  { topic: 'A small stall with excellent service', band: 3.5, cue: 'Describe a time when you received good service in an ordinary place.', points: ['where you were', 'what service you needed', 'what the person did', 'and explain why the service stood out'], answer: 'I received good service at a small fruit stall near a bus station. I was buying some fruit for my mother, who had just returned from hospital and did not want anything difficult to eat. I asked the seller which pears were ripe enough for that afternoon because I could not judge them well by looking.\n\nInstead of simply choosing the most expensive fruit, she explained the difference between two types and let me feel how firm they were. She selected three that were ready to eat and two that would be better after a couple of days. She put them in separate paper bags and wrote a short note on each so I would not mix them up. The purchase was small, and there were other customers waiting, but she still took the question seriously.\n\nThe service stood out because it solved my actual concern. I did not need a cheerful sales speech; I needed to avoid bringing home fruit my mother could not enjoy. The pears were suitable, and the simple labels made the purchase easy to use. I returned to the stall later, not because I expected special attention every time, but because the seller had shown that she knew her products and was willing to explain them honestly.' },
  { topic: 'A relative who changed their working life', band: 4, cue: 'Describe someone you know who made an important change to their work.', points: ['who the person is', 'what work they did before', 'what change they made', 'and explain what you admire about their approach'], answer: 'My cousin made an important change when he moved from selling office equipment to working in a plant nursery. He had spent several years visiting customers and preparing orders, but he increasingly disliked being indoors or in traffic for most of the day. He had always enjoyed growing plants at home, although he knew that a hobby was different from earning a living.\n\nRather than immediately leaving his job, he volunteered at the nursery on some weekends and asked about the difficult parts of the work. He learned that it involved lifting heavy trays, dealing with pests and working outside in uncomfortable weather. After saving enough money for a transition period, he applied for an assistant role and accepted that he would need to learn from people younger than him.\n\nI admire his approach because he took both his interest and the practical risks seriously. He did not describe the change as an easy escape from responsibility. During the first year, he was often tired and had much to learn, but he seemed more engaged when talking about his day. His example helped me see that making a change can require patience before and after the decision, rather than only courage at the moment of leaving.' },
  { topic: 'A gift made from a shared memory', band: 4.5, cue: 'Describe a gift you prepared yourself for another person.', points: ['who the gift was for', 'what you made', 'how you prepared it', 'and explain why it was suitable for that person'], answer: 'I prepared a small memory booklet for a friend who was moving abroad. We had met at college and spent several years exploring inexpensive places around the city together. I wanted to give her something light enough to pack that would still feel personal, so I chose a booklet rather than a large object.\n\nI selected ten photographs from our walks and printed them in a modest size. Under each picture, I wrote a short note about something funny or unexpected that had happened there. One page showed an empty bench because we had once arrived with a picnic just as the rain began. Another showed a café window where we had stayed for hours while planning an assignment. I left several blank pages at the back so she could add memories from her new home.\n\nThe gift was suitable because she enjoys keeping small records but dislikes clutter. It also recognised that moving away would create new experiences, not simply end the old ones. She laughed at the picnic page and said she had forgotten that incident. Making the booklet took longer than buying a present, but the process was enjoyable for me too. It made me appreciate the ordinary afternoons that had gradually become the substance of our friendship.' },
  { topic: 'Growing a first crop on a balcony', band: 5, cue: 'Describe something you grew or helped to grow.', points: ['what you grew', 'where you grew it', 'what you did to look after it', 'and explain what you learned from the experience'], answer: 'I grew a few tomato plants on our balcony last year. I had only kept decorative plants before, and I wanted to see whether we could produce something to eat in a small space. A neighbour gave me two young plants and advised me to use deeper containers than the ones I already had.\n\nI placed them where they received morning sunlight and checked the soil before watering. At first I watered too often because I assumed more attention meant better care. The leaves began to look weak, so I asked my neighbour and learned to let the top of the soil dry slightly. I also tied the stems loosely to supports as they grew. When flowers appeared, I became impatient, but the fruit took several more weeks to develop.\n\nWe eventually picked a small bowl of tomatoes, far fewer than a farm would produce but enough for a salad. The experience taught me to observe the plant’s condition rather than follow a rigid routine. It also made the time involved in growing food feel more real. I enjoyed the harvest partly because I remembered the mistakes and adjustments behind it. This year I will try again, but I will start with the same small number of plants rather than filling the whole balcony.' },
  { topic: 'Choosing a course after asking questions', band: 5.5, cue: 'Describe a decision you made after gathering information from several sources.', points: ['what you needed to decide', 'which sources you used', 'what information mattered most', 'and explain how you reached your decision'], answer: 'I needed to choose a beginner course in data analysis for work. Several advertisements promised quick results, and their titles sounded similar, so comparing prices alone did not seem useful. I wanted a course that would help me understand basic spreadsheets and charts rather than simply give me a certificate.\n\nI read the course outlines, spoke to a former learner and attended an online question session run by one provider. The most important information was how much practical feedback students received. One course had many recorded videos but little opportunity to ask questions. Another used fewer topics and required students to complete a small project with comments from the tutor. The former learner also warned me that some preparation was needed between classes, which helped me judge whether the schedule was realistic.\n\nI chose the project-based course even though it was slightly longer. My decision came from matching the teaching method to how I learn, not from assuming a longer or more expensive course must be better. I still found parts of it difficult, but I knew what support was available. Gathering information did not remove uncertainty completely; it gave me enough understanding to make a choice I could explain and commit time to.' },
  { topic: 'A sporting event watched from close by', band: 6, cue: 'Describe a sporting event that you watched in person.', points: ['what the event was', 'who you went with', 'what you noticed while watching', 'and explain whether the experience was worthwhile'], answer: 'I watched a local badminton tournament with a colleague who plays regularly. It took place in a sports hall rather than a large stadium, and we sat close enough to hear the players’ shoes on the floor. I knew the basic scoring rules, but I had never watched a competitive match from that distance.\n\nMy colleague explained why players sometimes hit the shuttle high rather than trying to finish the point immediately. Watching several rallies, I began to notice how each shot affected the opponent’s position. The speed was impressive, but the pauses between points were interesting too: players took a breath, looked across the court and seemed to reset their concentration. One match was close, and the spectators became very quiet during the final rallies.\n\nThe experience was worthwhile because I understood more about the skill involved than I usually do from brief television highlights. I also enjoyed supporting players who were not famous and seeing people from the local community gather around them. I did not suddenly become a serious player, but I tried badminton again afterwards with greater patience. I realised that keeping a rally going intelligently could be more satisfying than attempting a powerful shot I could not control.' },
  { topic: 'Learning to negotiate politely', band: 6.5, cue: 'Describe a time when you had to negotiate a practical arrangement.', points: ['what arrangement was needed', 'who you discussed it with', 'how you handled the discussion', 'and explain the outcome'], answer: 'I negotiated a delivery time for a washing machine when we moved into our current flat. The company initially offered a broad weekday window, but neither my partner nor I could stay home for the whole day. Cancelling the purchase would also have been inconvenient because we had already checked that the machine fitted the space.\n\nI called the delivery office and explained the constraint clearly. Instead of demanding an exact time they could not promise, I asked what alternatives were genuinely available. The staff member said the driver could call shortly before arriving, but that would not give me enough time to travel from work. We eventually found an early Saturday slot in the following week. I agreed to wait longer in exchange for a time we could manage, and she confirmed the arrangement by email.\n\nThe outcome was useful because it respected both sides’ practical limitations. I learned that negotiation does not always involve forcing someone to give up something; it can mean identifying which parts of an arrangement are flexible. The machine arrived within the revised window, and I thanked the staff member in a short reply. Being polite had not guaranteed success, but it made the conversation clear enough for us to discover an option that the original booking form had not shown.' },
  { topic: 'An animal observed behaving unexpectedly', band: 7, cue: 'Describe an occasion when you observed an animal doing something interesting.', points: ['which animal you saw', 'where you saw it', 'what it was doing', 'and explain why the behaviour interested you'], answer: 'I watched a small bird repeatedly carrying dry grass into a gap beneath a roof at our workplace. I noticed it while waiting outside for a colleague one morning. At first I thought it was simply picking up pieces from the ground, but it returned to the same gap several times with different lengths of material.\n\nI stood far enough away not to disturb it and watched how it approached. Sometimes it landed on a nearby wire before going to the roof; at other times it flew directly in. One piece of grass seemed too long, and it spent a while turning the material before disappearing into the opening. I assumed it was building a nest, but I did not try to inspect the gap because that would have interfered with whatever it was doing.\n\nThe behaviour interested me because the bird seemed to make small adjustments rather than follow exactly the same movement each time. I had walked past that building for months without noticing how animals used it. Later, I checked a reliable local wildlife guide to identify the bird more confidently. The observation was brief, but it changed my attention on the route to work. I now look for activity around ordinary structures while remembering that watching respectfully is more important than getting a perfect photograph.' },
  { topic: 'An audio book that fitted a busy week', band: 7.5, cue: 'Describe a recording you listened to that you found worthwhile.', points: ['what the recording was', 'when you listened to it', 'what you found interesting', 'and explain why that format suited you'], answer: 'I listened to an audio recording of a memoir about working in a public garden. I borrowed it through the library’s digital service during a week when I had several routine household tasks to finish. Reading a long book felt unlikely, but listening while folding clothes or preparing vegetables seemed possible.\n\nThe author described learning to notice changes in plants across the year, including the less attractive periods when beds looked empty or untidy. I found the discussion of patience particularly interesting. The work involved making decisions whose results would not be visible for months, which contrasted with my own tendency to expect an immediate sign of progress. The narrator used a calm voice without making every episode sound dramatic, so I could follow the details comfortably.\n\nThe audio format suited me because the chapters were clearly separated and I could stop after one without losing the thread. I did not listen during tasks that required close attention, since I wanted to understand the book rather than merely have sound in the background. It made a busy week more enjoyable, but I would not claim audio is always better than print. For this particular account, hearing the rhythm of the sentences added warmth and made ordinary maintenance feel unexpectedly absorbing.' },
  { topic: 'A quiz that rewarded different knowledge', band: 8, cue: 'Describe a competition or game in which different people contributed different strengths.', points: ['what the game or competition was', 'who took part with you', 'how people contributed', 'and explain what made the experience memorable'], answer: 'I took part in a community quiz with three colleagues. We joined mostly for an enjoyable evening, not because we expected to win. The questions covered local history, music, science and everyday practical knowledge, so no one person could answer everything confidently.\n\nOne colleague knew song introductions almost immediately, while another remembered dates and place names. I contributed more during a round involving misleading product labels, because interpreting small print was familiar from my work. The fourth member was excellent at noticing when we were becoming overconfident. She would ask which part of an answer we actually knew and which part we were guessing. We agreed to let the person with relevant knowledge explain before voting, rather than allowing the loudest suggestion to dominate.\n\nWe finished somewhere in the middle, but the event was memorable because it revealed strengths that were not obvious in our normal workplace roles. The cautious colleague, in particular, helped us avoid several plausible wrong answers. I also liked that our conversation afterwards focused on the strange facts we had learned rather than our position. The quiz illustrated a useful distinction between having knowledge and using it as a group: listening carefully and managing uncertainty mattered almost as much as recalling an answer quickly.' },
  { topic: 'A festival activity away from the main crowd', band: 3, cue: 'Describe an activity you enjoyed at a festival or public celebration.', points: ['what the celebration was', 'which activity you joined', 'what you did during it', 'and explain why you enjoyed that part'], answer: 'I enjoyed a paper lantern activity at a town festival last year. Most visitors were gathered around the main stage, where there was music and a large crowd. My cousin and I wanted a quieter place for a while, so we followed a sign to a craft tent beside the community hall.\n\nA volunteer gave us coloured paper, a safe small light and instructions for folding a lantern. We sat with a family whose children were making very different designs. I chose a simple pattern because I did not want to spend the whole session correcting mistakes. The volunteer showed me how to attach the bottom securely, then encouraged us to decorate the sides in our own way. My cousin drew birds, while I cut small shapes that let the light show through.\n\nI enjoyed that part because it allowed us to participate rather than only watch a performance. The atmosphere was friendly, and the task gave us an easy reason to talk to strangers at the table. We took the lanterns outside when it became darker and compared how they looked. I still have mine on a shelf, although the paper is slightly bent now. It reminds me of a calm and creative hour within a much louder celebration.' },
  { topic: 'An old letter found during a visit', band: 3.5, cue: 'Describe something from the past that you found unexpectedly.', points: ['what you found', 'where you found it', 'what you learned from it', 'and explain what you decided to do with it'], answer: 'I found an old letter while helping my aunt move some books into a new cupboard. It had been placed inside a dictionary and was written by her brother when he first left home for work. The envelope was very thin, and the stamp showed that it had travelled from a town much farther away than I had expected.\n\nMy aunt read it aloud after checking that it contained nothing too private. He described his small rented room, the difficulty of cooking for himself and his excitement about receiving his first pay. What interested me was the mixture of confidence and uncertainty. I had known him as a relaxed older man, so it was surprising to hear his younger voice worrying about ordinary tasks. The letter also mentioned a bakery that no longer existed near our family home.\n\nWe decided to keep the letter in a protective folder rather than return it to the book. With my aunt’s permission, I photographed both sides so other relatives could read it without handling the paper repeatedly. Finding it made family history feel more personal than a list of dates. It showed a moment when someone was learning how to live independently, which was something I could understand from my own experience.' },
  { topic: 'A meal shared with new neighbours', band: 4, cue: 'Describe a meal that helped you get to know someone better.', points: ['who you ate with', 'where the meal took place', 'what you talked about', 'and explain why the meal helped your relationship'], answer: 'I shared a meal with two new neighbours shortly after they moved into our apartment building. We had already exchanged greetings near the lift, but we knew very little about each other. They invited us to a simple dinner on their balcony, where there was enough room for four people around a small table.\n\nThe food was easy to share: rice, vegetables and a fish dish that one of them had learned from his father. We talked first about practical things, such as nearby shops and the building’s recycling system. Then the conversation moved to the places where we had grown up. One neighbour explained why he missed living near water, and I realised we both enjoyed walking along the river in the early morning. There were also a few comfortable pauses while we served food, so nobody had to keep the conversation going continuously.\n\nThe meal helped because we met outside the hurried setting of the corridor. We could ask questions and listen without worrying about holding the lift doors open. Afterwards, greeting them felt natural rather than formal, and we occasionally exchanged useful information about the area. The dinner did not instantly create a close friendship, but it gave us a friendly foundation for living alongside one another.' },
  { topic: 'A small catalogue made for a shared cupboard', band: 4.5, cue: 'Describe a project that made a shared resource easier to use.', points: ['what the resource was', 'what problem existed', 'what you did', 'and explain how people responded'], answer: 'I helped create a simple catalogue for a cupboard of books in our community centre. People could borrow the books freely, but the shelves had become disorganised and nobody knew which books were missing. Some visitors assumed there was nothing suitable because interesting titles were hidden behind stacks of magazines.\n\nTwo volunteers and I took the books out one shelf at a time. We removed damaged duplicates only after checking with the centre manager, then grouped the remaining books into broad subjects. I entered the titles into a shared spreadsheet and made a printed list for visitors who did not use a phone. We also added a notebook where borrowers could record a book’s title and their expected return date. The system was deliberately simple because nobody could manage a complicated database every day.\n\nPeople responded positively, especially when they discovered books they had not noticed before. A few visitors suggested additional categories, and we changed the labels accordingly. The project did not prevent every late return, but it made the cupboard more welcoming and easier to maintain. I learned that improving a shared resource often means reducing small points of confusion, not introducing the most advanced possible technology.' },
  { topic: 'Borrowing clothes for an important occasion', band: 5, cue: 'Describe an occasion when you used something borrowed instead of buying something new.', points: ['what the occasion was', 'what you borrowed', 'who lent it to you', 'and explain why borrowing was a sensible choice'], answer: 'I borrowed a formal jacket for a college award ceremony. I needed to look reasonably smart, but I rarely attended formal events and could not justify buying a jacket that might remain in the cupboard for years. My brother had one that was close to my size and offered to let me try it.\n\nThe sleeves were slightly long, so we checked whether folding the cuffs neatly would work without altering the jacket. I wore it with plain trousers I already owned and a clean shirt. Before the event, I asked whether my brother needed it during the same week, because I did not want the loan to cause him difficulty. I kept it away from food while travelling and hung it properly when I returned home.\n\nBorrowing was sensible because it met a temporary need without creating an unnecessary purchase. The jacket was comfortable enough for the evening, and nobody was concerned that it was not new. I returned it after arranging cleaning in the way my brother preferred. The experience also made me less anxious about preparing for formal occasions. I realised that looking appropriate did not require buying a complete outfit, and asking a family member for help could be practical rather than embarrassing.' },
  { topic: 'An explanation using a simple demonstration', band: 5.5, cue: 'Describe a demonstration that helped you understand a scientific idea.', points: ['which idea was explained', 'where you saw the demonstration', 'what the presenter did', 'and explain why the demonstration was effective'], answer: 'A demonstration at a public science afternoon helped me understand why different materials insulate heat differently. It took place in a library meeting room, and the presenter used ordinary cups rather than complicated equipment. I had heard the word insulation many times but had not thought carefully about what it meant in everyday objects.\n\nShe poured equally warm water into three similar cups and wrapped each in a different material. We checked their temperatures at regular intervals and wrote the readings on a board. Before seeing the results, the audience predicted which cup would stay warm longest and explained why. The presenter also pointed out that we had to keep the starting temperature and amount of water comparable; otherwise, the comparison would be unfair.\n\nThe demonstration was effective because the change happened slowly enough for us to observe and discuss it. I could connect the idea with a flask, a jacket and the walls of a house without assuming they all worked identically. I also appreciated that one unexpected reading led to checking the measurement rather than pretending the result was perfect. It showed both the scientific principle and the importance of a careful method. Afterwards, I understood more clearly why testing a claim requires controlling the conditions, not only seeing an interesting outcome.' },
  { topic: 'A walk without a phone', band: 6, cue: 'Describe a time when you deliberately did an activity without using your phone.', points: ['what the activity was', 'why you left your phone aside', 'what you noticed during the activity', 'and explain how you felt about the experience'], answer: 'I deliberately went for a short walk without my phone after a week of checking messages constantly. I was not trying to prove that phones were bad; I simply wanted to see whether I could enjoy half an hour without turning every pause into an opportunity to read something. I left it charging at home and chose a route I knew well.\n\nAt first I felt that something was missing from my pocket. I also noticed how often I would normally photograph a view rather than just look at it. After a few minutes, my attention settled on smaller details, including a damaged pavement tile and the sound of someone practising a musical instrument behind an open window. I took a slightly longer route because I did not need to check the time precisely.\n\nThe experience felt restful, but I would not do it in every situation. A phone is useful for unfamiliar routes or when someone may need to contact me urgently. For a familiar local walk, leaving it aside removed a small pressure to keep responding. I returned with no dramatic discovery, just a clearer sense that attention can be directed deliberately. Since then, I sometimes silence the phone and keep it in my bag, which provides a similar break while retaining its practical usefulness.' },
  { topic: 'Helping at an accessible reading event', band: 6.5, cue: 'Describe a time when you volunteered to help at an event.', points: ['what the event was', 'what help was needed', 'what you did', 'and explain what the experience taught you'], answer: 'I volunteered at a community reading event that offered large-print books and spoken extracts for people who found ordinary print difficult. The organiser needed volunteers to guide visitors between tables and explain the timetable. I had no specialist knowledge, so my role was practical rather than instructional.\n\nBefore the event, we received a short briefing about asking people what assistance they wanted instead of immediately taking control. I helped place clear signs, kept the walking routes free of bags and described where each session would take place. One visitor asked me to read a timetable quietly, while another preferred that I simply point out the largest-print copy. The different requests showed why assuming everyone needed the same help would have been inappropriate.\n\nThe experience taught me that accessibility involves both physical arrangements and respectful communication. Moving a chair or slowing down an explanation can matter, but the person receiving assistance should still make their own decisions. I also noticed that many improvements, such as clearer signs, helped everyone in the room. I left feeling useful without imagining I had solved a complex issue in one afternoon. I would volunteer again, especially for a role where I could learn the practical requirements beforehand and avoid creating extra work for the organiser.' },
  { topic: 'A short ferry trip with a different perspective', band: 7, cue: 'Describe a journey that let you see a familiar area from a new perspective.', points: ['how you travelled', 'which area you passed through', 'what looked different', 'and explain why the journey was memorable'], answer: 'I took a short ferry journey across the river that runs through my city. I had crossed the same stretch many times by bridge, usually while concentrating on traffic. A friend suggested the ferry because we had no urgent appointment and could make the crossing part of an afternoon out.\n\nFrom the water, the riverfront buildings seemed less imposing than they did from the road. I could see the spaces between them and notice which entrances actually faced the river. The underside of the bridge also revealed pipes and structural details that were invisible from above. As the ferry moved slowly away from the bank, I saw people walking along a path I had assumed ended much earlier. The journey lasted only around fifteen minutes, but there was enough time to observe without hurrying.\n\nIt was memorable because it changed the relationship between familiar landmarks. Places that seemed separate in my normal routes became parts of one continuous waterfront. I did not need to travel far to experience that difference; changing the mode of travel was enough. Afterwards, we walked along the path I had noticed from the boat. The trip reminded me that knowing an area through one routine can leave important gaps, and a modest change of perspective can make it feel newly interesting.' },
  { topic: 'Checking a dramatic claim before sharing it', band: 7.5, cue: 'Describe a time when you checked information before passing it on.', points: ['what the information claimed', 'where you first saw it', 'how you checked it', 'and explain what you decided to do'], answer: 'I checked a message claiming that all local buses would stop running the following morning. It appeared in a neighbourhood group during a period of roadworks, so it sounded plausible. Several people were already discussing alternative journeys, and I was tempted to forward it to a colleague who depended on the bus.\n\nBefore sharing it, I looked at the transport operator’s website and its official service notices. There was a disruption, but it affected two routes during specific hours rather than the whole network. I also noticed that the shared image had been cropped so the date and route numbers were missing. To make sure I was not relying on an outdated page, I checked the latest notice and the time it had been posted.\n\nI decided not to forward the original message. Instead, I sent my colleague the official link and explained which routes were affected. In the group, I added the same information without accusing anyone of deliberately misleading others. The experience mattered because a partly accurate claim can be more convincing than a completely invented one. I learned to look for scope, date and source, especially when a message creates urgency. Checking took a few minutes, but it prevented me from contributing to confusion that could have disrupted many people’s morning plans.' },
  { topic: 'Advice about declining an extra commitment', band: 8, cue: 'Describe advice that helped you make a difficult personal decision.', points: ['what decision you faced', 'who gave the advice', 'what they suggested', 'and explain how the advice influenced you'], answer: 'I faced a decision about joining an additional project at work while preparing for an important examination. The project interested me, and I worried that declining would make me seem unhelpful. At the same time, I was already struggling to give proper attention to the responsibilities I had accepted.\n\nA former supervisor advised me to distinguish between being willing to help and promising time I did not realistically have. She suggested writing down the existing commitments and considering what would have to be reduced if I accepted another one. She also said I could decline the full role while offering a limited contribution, provided that contribution was clearly defined and genuinely manageable. The advice did not tell me which ambition should matter most; it gave me a way to examine the consequences honestly.\n\nI eventually explained my situation to the project leader and offered to review one short document rather than join the ongoing meetings. He accepted that arrangement without the disappointment I had imagined. The advice influenced me by making a boundary feel like responsible planning rather than selfishness. I still believe opportunities deserve serious consideration, but I am more cautious about commitments that sound attractive only because I have not counted their full cost in time and attention.' },
  { topic: 'A promise kept over several weeks', band: 8, cue: 'Describe a promise that required you to follow through over time.', points: ['what you promised', 'who the promise was made to', 'what you did to keep it', 'and explain why keeping it mattered'], answer: 'I promised my younger brother that I would practise presentation skills with him once a week before his first college assessment. He was anxious about speaking in front of people and wanted someone familiar to listen without interrupting. It would have been easy to encourage him vaguely, but a regular session seemed more useful.\n\nWe agreed on a short Sunday evening meeting and kept it to twenty minutes so it would remain manageable. He practised one part of the presentation while I noted places where the explanation was unclear. I asked him which kind of feedback he wanted before commenting, because too many corrections at once made him lose confidence. One week I had to travel, so we used a video call rather than cancelling. Another week he was tired, and we focused only on the opening.\n\nKeeping the promise mattered because it showed that his concern was worth consistent attention. His final presentation was not flawless, but he could recover after losing a sentence and continue explaining the topic. I was also pleased that we had found a supportive routine without making me responsible for the whole assessment. The experience reminded me that a modest promise kept reliably can be more valuable than an ambitious offer that becomes difficult to maintain.' },
];
